<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bede</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 673–735</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as the Venerable Bede, Saint Bede, Venerable Bede</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bede was a Northumbrian monk and scholar of the twin monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Wearmouth–Jarrow</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastical-history-of-the-english-people">Ecclesiastical History of the English People</a>, completed in 731, is the principal source for the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdoms and their conversion, and his works on chronology popularised dating events from the birth of Christ. Known as the Venerable Bede, he was declared a Doctor of the Church in 1899.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bede.jpg" alt="Bede" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: e-codices.unifr.ch · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E-codices_bke-0047_001v_medium_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 603, however, his large army was crushed by Æthelfrith of Northumbria at the Battle of Degsastan, after which, <b>Bede</b> wrote, no king of the Scots in Britain dared to make war on the English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first Mercian king named by <b>Bede</b> is Cearl, whose daughter married Edwin of Northumbria in the early 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Northumbrian power was won by war. Æthelfrith, Edwin, Oswald and Oswiu each held a wide overlordship backed by royal war bands, and <b>Bede</b> counts Edwin, Oswald and Oswiu among the kings who held imperium over the southern English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 615 Æthelfrith of Northumbria defeated the Britons at the Battle of Chester, where King Selyf ap Cynan of Powys was killed and, according to <b>Bede</b>, about 1,200 monks of Bangor-on-Dee who had come to pray for the British army were slaughtered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bede</b> reports that the Picts chose their kings from the female line when the succession was in doubt, and many Pictish kings had fathers who were not kings, so some historians argue for a matrilineal system, while others doubt it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ceawlin, who ruled in the later sixth century and was listed by <b>Bede</b> among the kings who held authority over the southern English, expanded westwards; the Chronicle records his victory at the Battle of Dyrham in 577, which brought Gloucester, Cirencester and Bath under West Saxon control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
